---
import ProductLayout from '../../layouts/ProductLayout.astro';

const live = 'https://lock-in-study-app.vercel.app/';
const img = (f: string) => `/images/lockin/${f}`;
---
<ProductLayout
  slug="lock-in"
  section="playground"
  eyebrow="Vibe coding · Product design"
  title="Lock In"
  tagline="<strong>A study app that turns your classes into 15-minute exam games. I designed it and built it by directing AI.</strong> Not a published app. A working demo."
  hero={img('desk-06-today.webp')}
  heroAlt="Lock In Today screen: a gray card that says Let's lock in today with a Start Daily Session button, the star mascot, an Up next list of exams and mastery rings per course"
  links={[['Live demo →', live]]}
  overview={[['Role', 'Product idea, UX + UI design, prompt writing, AI direction, review, deployment'], ['Timeline', '2 days'], ['Tools', 'Google AI Studio (Gemini), Claude Code, React, Tailwind, Vercel']]}
>
  <section class="chapter">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p class="lead">Exam prep should feel like playing, not studying.</p>
    <p>Studying for exams meant <strong>re-prompting ChatGPT</strong> over and over. Paste notes. Explain the class. Ask for a quiz. Do it again tomorrow. Why re-prompt every night?</p>
    <p>I wanted <strong>one stop for exam prep</strong>. Set it up once, then just play.</p>
    <p>The research backs the format. Gloria Mark found people now stay on one screen for about <strong>47 seconds</strong> before switching (<em>Attention Span</em>, 2023). Long study sessions fight that. Short rounds work with it.</p>
    <p>Games also make you recall, not reread. Roediger and Karpicke (2006) found that <strong>testing yourself</strong> beats rereading for long-term memory. Dunlosky and colleagues (2013) rated practice testing and spaced practice the two study methods with <strong>high utility</strong>.</p>
    <p><strong>Who it’s for:</strong> college students, starting with me. They open it for about 15 minutes a day and need to know what to do next within 2 seconds.</p>
    <p><strong>How it works:</strong> connect Canvas or upload slides and past exams. Lock In forecasts the topics most likely to be on your exam. It turns them into short games, inspired by Duolingo and Quizlet, aimed at what you haven’t mastered.</p>
    <div class="learn"><b>What I learned</b><p>The best feature removes the step you repeat most.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
    <h2>Version one</h2>
    <p class="lead">It worked. It didn’t feel like mine.</p>
    <p>Day 1, I vibe coded the first version in <strong>Google AI Studio</strong> with Gemini. The features worked. The interface felt generic and AI-made: an olive and lime theme, emoji everywhere, and a top bar with <strong>12 controls</strong> competing for attention.</p>
    <p>It was fine, but it didn’t meet my expectations. On day 2 I moved to <strong>Claude Code</strong>, because I understood the process better there.</p>
    <div class="band cols">
      <figure><img src={img('before-02-dashboard.webp')} alt="Version one dashboard in olive and lime, with emoji icons and a crowded top bar of controls" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Before: the AI Studio dashboard.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src={img('before-03-game.webp')} alt="Version one game screen in olive and lime with emoji and stacked answer buttons" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Before: the AI Studio game.</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
    <div class="learn"><b>What I learned</b><p>AI can build features. It can’t build taste.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
    <h2>Design</h2>
    <p>I started on <strong>Pinterest</strong>, my go-to app for inspiration. I pinned a character-led fitness app, <strong>Marshmallow</strong>’s friendly insurance screens and <strong>Duolingo</strong>.</p>
    <figure class="band"><img src={img('inspiration.webp')} alt="Pinterest inspiration board: a fitness app led by a star character, soft Marshmallow insurance screens, and Duolingo lessons" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Inspiration I pulled from Pinterest.</figcaption></figure>
    <p>Then I made mockups for <strong>5 screens</strong>: Connect, Today, Forecast, Game and Results. Plus a navigation map. The rule: <strong>every path ends back on Today</strong>, so there’s always one clear next step.</p>
    <figure class="band"><img src={img('nav-map.webp')} alt="Navigation map: set up once (welcome, pick a source, confirm exams, semester scope), a daily loop (Today, start session, game, results) and a before-exam path (forecast, play top 3 topics), all returning to Today" loading="lazy" /></figure>
    <div class="band cols">
      <figure><img src={img('mockup-today.webp')} alt="My Today mockup: a black and white dashboard with one Start Daily Session button and the tan star mascot" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Mockup: Today.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src={img('mockup-game.webp')} alt="My game mockup: a question card next to the star mascot with four numbered answers in a grid" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Mockup: Game.</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
    <div class="learn"><b>What I learned</b><p>One clear next step beats ten options.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
    <h2>The prompt</h2>
    <p class="lead">I wrote the brief, not the code.</p>
    <p>I wrote a structured redesign prompt: <strong>role, context, audience, task, design spec, constraints</strong>. I directed the AI with a spec, not vibes. These lines did the most work:</p>
    <ul class="brief">
      <li>Keep every existing feature and all of the data logic.</li>
      <li>Every screen has exactly one primary action.</li>
      <li>Meet WCAG AA contrast.</li>
      <li>Works at 390px and 1280px with no sideways scrolling.</li>
      <li>Pause for my review after each step.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="learn"><b>What I learned</b><p>I didn’t write code. I wrote the rules it had to follow.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
    <h2>The rebuild</h2>
    <p class="lead">Claude Code rebuilt it on my design.</p>
    <p>It read the code first and <strong>mapped every old screen</strong> to my mockups. Then it rebuilt the UI on <strong>design tokens</strong>, fixed bugs, made it installable on a phone and deployed it.</p>
    <div class="band cols">
      <figure><img src={img('before-02-dashboard.webp')} alt="Before: the olive and lime AI Studio dashboard" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Before</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src={img('desk-06-today.webp')} alt="After: the white Today screen with one black Start Daily Session button and the tan star" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>After</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
    <div class="band cols">
      <figure><img src={img('before-03-game.webp')} alt="Before: the AI Studio game with emoji and stacked answers" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Before</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src={img('desk-08-game.webp')} alt="After: a focused game screen with a progress bar, hearts, the star and four numbered answer cards" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>After</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
    <div class="band bigstats four after-stats">
      <div><b>8,700</b><span>lines in the original export, about</span></div>
      <div><b>400 → 20</b><span>hardcoded colors to design tokens</span></div>
      <div><b>12 → 6</b><span>top-bar controls to nav items</span></div>
      <div><b>9</b><span>screens redesigned</span></div>
    </div>
    <p><strong>The design system:</strong> black, white and gray, Inter type, 16px card corners and pill buttons. One accent: black. The <strong>star mascot</strong> in tan is the only color in the app.</p>
    <div class="band cols">
      <figure><img src={img('desk-02-source.webp')} alt="Setup screen asking Where should I study from, with Sync with Canvas selected and Upload files as the other option" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Setup: pick a source.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src={img('desk-07-forecast.webp')} alt="Forecast screen listing likely exam topics with likelihood bars, a professor guidance card and the sources it was built from" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Forecast: what’s likely on the exam.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src={img('desk-09-feedback.webp')} alt="Game feedback bar along the bottom with an explanation of the answer and a Continue button" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Feedback after each answer.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src={img('desk-10-results.webp')} alt="Results screen with the star, exam readiness rising from 78 to 80 percent, and mastery by topic" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Results: readiness 78% → 80%.</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
    <div class="learn"><b>What I learned</b><p>Tokens turn 400 decisions into 20.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
    <h2>Proof</h2>
    <p class="lead">My constraints became the test plan.</p>
    <p>Every requirement in my prompt became a check: screenshots at <strong>phone and laptop widths</strong>, an <strong>automated accessibility audit</strong> and a production build test.</p>
    <div class="band bigstats">
      <div><b>7</b><span>bugs found and fixed</span></div>
      <div><b>0</b><span>WCAG AA issues across every screen and modal</span></div>
      <div><b>$0</b><span>hosting, on the Vercel and Gemini free tiers</span></div>
    </div>
    <h3>The 7 bugs</h3>
    <ul class="list">
      <li>npm install failed</li>
      <li>Mastery didn’t save on reload</li>
      <li>Custom and uploaded classes couldn’t start a game</li>
      <li>The streak never passed 1 day</li>
      <li>XP history piled every day into one entry</li>
      <li>The settings modal saved stale data</li>
      <li>The Vercel build failed on TypeScript 7</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="learn"><b>What I learned</b><p>If I can’t check it, I didn’t really ask for it.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
    <h2>Try it</h2>
    <p class="lead">A live demo you can play with.</p>
    <p>It’s <strong>not a published product</strong>. It’s not in an app store, it runs on free tiers, and your data stays in your browser. Tap <strong>Try it with sample classes</strong> to see everything right away.</p>
    <div class="band phones">
      <figure><img src={img('phone-06-today.webp')} alt="Lock In Today screen on a phone, with the daily session button and Up next exams stacked" loading="lazy" /></figure>
      <figure><img src={img('phone-08-game.webp')} alt="Lock In game on a phone, with the question card and four answer cards stacked" loading="lazy" /></figure>
    </div>
    <div class="install">
      <img src={img('app-icon.webp')} alt="Lock In app icon: the smiling tan star on white" width="72" height="72" loading="lazy" />
      <p><strong>Install it:</strong> open the demo on your phone, tap Share, then <strong>Add to Home Screen</strong>.</p>
    </div>
    <h3>What’s next</h3>
    <ul class="list">
      <li>PDF uploads only send the file name to the AI right now. Reading the text inside them is next.</li>
      <li>Canvas connects and pulls your course list, but doesn’t create classes yet. Automatic sync is next.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="learn"><b>What I learned</b><p>Ship the demo, then say honestly what’s missing.</p></div>
    <p class="ctas"><a class="btn" href={live} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try the live demo →</a></p>
  </section>
</ProductLayout>

<style>
  .chapter h3 { margin: 32px 0 8px; font-size: 1.3rem; letter-spacing: -0.02em; }
  .brief { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 16px 0 0; display: grid; gap: 8px; max-width: var(--text); }
  .brief li { padding: 12px 16px; border-left: 3px solid var(--ink); background: var(--white); font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace; font-size: 0.92rem; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--ink); }
  .list { padding-left: 1.2em; margin: 8px 0 0; color: var(--ink-2); font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.6; max-width: var(--text); }
  .list li { margin-bottom: 4px; }
  .after-stats { margin-block: 28px; }
  .after-stats b { font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 4.6vw, 3.6rem); white-space: nowrap; }
  .cols figure img { border: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .phones { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 260px)); gap: 20px; }
  .phones img { border: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .install { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 16px; margin-top: 24px; max-width: var(--text); }
  .install img { width: 72px; height: 72px; border-radius: 18px; border: 1px solid var(--line); flex: none; }
  .install p { margin: 0; }
  .ctas { display: flex; gap: 10px; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-top: 28px !important; }
</style>
